==Old English==
===Etymology===
From Germanic: cognate with Old Saxon ''unnan'', Old High German ''unnan'', Middle Dutch ''[[onnen]]'', Old Norse ''[[unna]]'' (Icelandic, Norwegian, Swedish ''[[unna]]'', Danish ''[[unde]]'').

===Pronunciation===
{{IPA|/ˈunːɑn/}}

===Verb===
'''unnan''' (preterite-present: ''third-person sg present'' '''[[ann]]'''/'''[[onn]]''', ''third-person sg preterite'' {{Latinx|'''[[uþe|ūþe]]'''}}, ''preterite plural'' {{Latinx|'''[[uþon|ūþon]]'''}}, ''past participle'' ('''ġe''')'''[[unnen]]''')

# to [[grant]] or [[bestow]] (+ ''dat'' (person) + ''gen'' (thing))
# to [[wish]]
# to [[intercede]]

====Descendants====
*Middle English: [[unne]]

